# q10 — DELIBERATE ERROR. The consumer supplies a method of its own with
# the same name as one an imported `extend` already gave the same type.
#
# `megaphone.lume` line 10 writes `extend Str with Loud:`, whose `shout`
# (line 11) becomes a method of `Str` in every file that imports it.
# This file writes `extend Str with Polite:` — a *different* interface,
# so it is not the (type, interface) pair the clash rule forbids — whose
# `shout` becomes a second method of `Str` with the same name and the
# same signature.
#
# Meant to be REFUSED.
#
# EXPECTED ERROR: a duplicate-method error on `Str`, reported at the
# second `extend`'s method, `main.lume:42`, and naming the first,
# `megaphone.lume:11`, the way the two-extends rule names both files.
# Second best: an ambiguity error at the call, `main.lume:45`, saying
# `Str.shout` could be `Loud.shout` or `Polite.shout`.
#
# Why I think it must be refused rather than resolved: the README's one
# tie-break is "a method of your own wins over the built-in of the same
# name", which is about built-ins, not about two extends. Letting the
# nearer file win would silently change what `megaphone.announce` does
# for everyone, since `announce` calls `x.shout` through the `Loud`
# bound; letting the library win would silently ignore what this file
# wrote. Both are worse than an error.
#
# If it is accepted instead, the two outcomes to tell apart are `HELLO!`
# (the library's `shout` won) and `hello...` (this file's won) on the
# first line of output.

import megaphone
import poster

interface Polite:
  def shout -> Str

  def softly -> Str = "(#{shout})"

# A DIFFERENT interface, so not the pair the clash rule names — but a
# second `shout` on `Str` all the same.
extend Str with Polite:
  def shout -> Str = "#{downcase}..."

def main:
  puts "Hello".shout
  puts "Hello".twice
  puts "Hello".softly
  puts megaphone.announce("Hello")
  puts poster.banner(["one", "two"])
